\section{Reading your own log as numbers}
\label{tut:metrics}

This tutorial turns the recorded project back on its author.
The previous tutorials read the log as a sequence---play it back, jump
to a tag---and this one reads it as summaries: how many days and runs,
how often an error was followed by the same error, how large and how
branched the program is, and whether any of that is moving.
The invariant is the same recorded project; what varies across the
steps is the \emph{question} asked of it, one metric family at a time,
so the learner can attribute each new number to the question that
produced it rather than to a change in the data.

Two design choices shape every step.
First, the learner makes the history before measuring it: the opening
step has them run a program that fails twice in the same way and then
succeeds, so that every later number has a visible cause the learner
remembers typing.
Second, each step asks for a prediction before the command---how many
sessions, which error series will be higher, whether the trend is
decidable yet---because a number the learner has guessed at first is
one they read, and a number that merely appears is one they skim.
The order runs from the metric with the most obvious meaning (days and
runs) to the one that needs the most caveats (trends), and ends where
the playback tutorial ended: in [[learnlog list]], seeing that the
reflection itself was recorded.

\subsection{Two variants, one contract}

As in \cref{tut:getting-started}, the tutorial tangles into a novice
entry, [[metrics]], and an expert entry, [[metrics-expert]], sharing
every step's checks through common chunks.
The mechanism the expert variant makes visible is the definition
behind each number: what a session is and where the twenty minutes
come from, which pairs of runs the error quotient scores, which tool
counts the complexity and why the maintainability index carries a
warning, how a linter is found, and what rule turns a series into the
word \enquote{rising}.
The novice variant keeps to reading the tables and never names a
paper or a tool.

